WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.530
- Artificial intelligence.
- Jobs.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.060
- Artificial intelligence.
- Job.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.960
- Artificial intelligence.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.030
- [Joanna] You've heard the AI warnings.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.970
- Every technological revolution
affects the job market.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.180
- AI could cost some humans
their jobs in the near future.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.770
- But AI is also creating some
new jobs, specifically one.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.753
So I decided to apply.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.600
Job title: prompt engineer.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.240
Responsibilities: writing text commands

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.730
or prompts to get the best answers out

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.273
of a generative AI large
language model like ChatGPT.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.200
Pay: up to $250,000 a year.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.180
To figure out exactly what
a prompt engineer does,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.940
I decided to apply to be one at Hebbia,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.713
a New York-based AI startup.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:45.483
Wish me luck.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.623
Hi
- Swetha. Good to meet you.

00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:50.456
- Nice to meet.
- Welcome to the office.

00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:52.320
- I have wanted to be a prompt engineer

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.777
as long as I can remember.

00:00:54.777 --> 00:00:55.610
(rewinding sound)

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.300
All right, fine, the company was in

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.710
on this little experiment.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.180
- It's really interesting
that you say that

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.160
because the role actually didn't exist

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.470
until maybe a year ago.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.880
- [Joanna] Yes, prompt engineering

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.680
is a totally new type of job

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.600
because well, large language models

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.600
or LLMs, like ChatGPT only
hit the scene a year ago.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.470
And if you've used ChatGPT,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.190
prompt engineering is actually
pretty easy to understand.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.590
You type in your prompt,
instructing whatever you'd like

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.570
the large language model to do.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.820
The model then returns an answer,

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.130
but sometimes that answer is unwieldy

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.080
or not what you wanted.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.500
The better the input,
the better the output.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.290
- For really one of the first times ever,

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:41.310
we've been able to expose
a pretty complicated piece

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.470
of technology to someone

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.900
that doesn't know how to code,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.160
to someone that knows how
to talk to these models

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.000
in a highly instructive, highly clear way.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.190
And that person is the prompt engineer.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.810
- So really it's a translator.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.250
- That's exactly what it is.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.040
You're translating between
the machine and the human.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.870
- Seems like something I
could be pretty good at.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:04.830
- I think so too.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.690
- But what's a good prompt

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.790
and how do you learn to write them?

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:12.240
Well, I went back to school and by school,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.399
I mean a Coursera class.

00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:16.020
(playful music)

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.810
Look at that. 100 and 100.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.640
- Prompts aren't just a one-off

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.950
that you give to the large language model.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.040
- In his six-week prompt
engineering for ChatGPT class,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.810
Dr. Jules White, a professor

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.870
of computer science at
Vanderbilt University teaches you

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.400
how to structure sentences

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.740
to get the desired output from the bot.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.840
You seem bored by this, Browser.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.780
There's the persona or role prompt.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.580
For instance, act as an assistant

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.860
helping a financial analyst.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:47.550
You're instructing the
LLM to take on a persona.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.830
There's the new information prompt.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.900
This is where you feed the model new data

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.160
it might not know about.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.030
Say, here's the transcript from
the Microsoft earnings call.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.700
Please summarize the mentions of Bing,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.803
and you get a summary.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.180
Hebbia's Matrix app
actually does all of this.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.890
It allows financial institutions

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.690
and other businesses to upload

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.390
or access huge datasets, filings,

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.490
earnings transcripts, calls.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.970
Then it uses AI to find
answers so people don't have

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.220
to manually go through it all.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.900
And that's what Swetha gave me

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.000
as a prompt engineering assignment.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.950
- So in this case, let's say
that you are doing an analysis

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.710
on earnings calls for a public company.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.840
- I did a pretty good job
at writing those prompts,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:34.673
but there was another part

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:38.130
of the job I wasn't prepared for: coding.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.230
In some cases, a prompt engineer may have

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.990
to code the queries into the tool itself

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:46.410
so Hebbia's clients can
use them with a single tap.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.690
It was about that time
that I knew this probably

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.103
wasn't my next job.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.790
Plus, I was a little worried

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.090
about how long this job might
be a job, which I decided

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.430
to bring up in my interview with the CEO.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.350
Joanna. Nice to meet you.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.360
As these models get better,

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.570
is prompt engineering gonna be necessary?

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.700
- At the end of the day,
you'll still need people

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.593
to tell them explicit instructions.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.840
Even in programming

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.110
where machines are perfect
at understanding code,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.193
there's still a job for
people to go back and forth.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.670
- So what did I learn today?

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:24.930
Well, getting a job

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.080
as a prompt engineer is
harder than I thought.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.800
Still, anyone who wants to
successfully work with AI tools

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.170
and elicit the best answers from them

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.740
really should learn some of
these prompt writing skills,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.330
even if your next job title

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.333
isn't officially prompt engineer.

00:04:43.225 --> 00:04:44.310
(Joanna sighing)

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.590
Joanna, enjoyed meeting you too.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:47.910
After some deliberation,

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:51.000
we will not be continuing
the hiring process here.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.253
Wishing you the best in your search.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:55.833
Yeah, rough.

